WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:04.220
Hey Wisecrack.

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:05.220
Jared again.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.480
While you probably think of Key &amp; Peele as
the duo that gave us President Obama’s anger

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:13.340
translator, Luther or the visionaries that
finally made an action movie about a kitten

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.420
they’re also responsible for some of the
smartest comedy that’s recently graced the

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:18.100
airwaves.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.460
And while much of their success can be chalked
up to their unique, and often absurd, characters.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.949
Their true brilliance lies in their streamlined
writing style, and a commitment to sketches

00:00:27.949 --> 00:00:29.100
that are as funny

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.120
as they are socially and
emotionally true.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.200
And how do they tell that truth? Well, let's find out.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.040
Since nothing is better than over-explaining a joke. Welcome to thiss Wisecrack Edition on Key &amp; Peele.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.680
And I guess there's sort of spoilers ahead?

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.460
To really understand what makes Key &amp; Peele
so great, we need to look a bit deeper and

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.410
discover the comedic philosophy that informs
their work.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:59.060
And believe it or not, these guys follow in
the footsteps of this guy.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.300
For those of you unfamiliar, that isn’t
the demon lovechild of Dan Harmon, Alan Moore,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.750
and Slavoj Zizek - that’s Del Close, a pioneer
of comedy who is still hugely influential

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:13.420
today.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:17.851
But before we get into how Key &amp; Peele embody
Close’s comedic philosophy, we need to take

00:01:17.851 --> 00:01:20.300
a look at a little comedy science.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:25.100
While you probably don’t associate science
with comedy, we can understand the success

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.140
of many Key &amp; Peele sketches by distilling
them down to a core comedic structure.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:35.660
In this structure, we start in a world with a built-in set of expectations which is then contrasted with an

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:41.320
unusual behavior. And this behavior is heightened and explored until you've pissed yourself from

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.520
laughing so hard.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.950
In other words, comedy is arrived at through
exploring a behavior that breaks with the

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.240
expectations we have of a given situation.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.909
If we wanted to go full ‘Beautiful Mind’
on this structure, it might look a little

00:01:53.909 --> 00:02:00.020
something like this: Reasonable Reality and
Set of Expectations (+) Unusual Behavior and/or

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:05.980
Point of View (x) Raised Emotional Stakes
(+) Ending Which Subverts Established Pattern

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.349
= Comedic Bliss.

00:02:08.349 --> 00:02:12.730
But enough comedic calculus, let’s look
at, ‘Substitute Teacher’ as an example.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.950
The sketch opens in a high school classroom,
an environment that carries with it a set

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:22.120
of standard expectations: bored students,
chalkboards, books, and a teacher trying to

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.330
wield some authority.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.620
But this all changes when Mr. Garvey begins
taking attendance.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.740
Now that we have our unusual behavior, i.e.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:45.630
Mr. Garvey’s inability to pronounce stereotypically
white names, things can really get cooking.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:53.459
We get to see both the logic behind Mr. Garvey’s
frustration along with the increasingly terrified

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:54.459
students.

00:02:54.459 --> 00:03:00.600
As Mr. Garvey continues this unusual behavior,
his anger increases, while their fear heightens.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.180
This simple underlying logic is what helps
accelerate the comedy with each move.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:09.960
Once we see this pattern repeated a few times,
we are already predicting the next move.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.560
But not to be bested by an attentive audience,
the sketch ends by flipping our expectations.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.700
For once, Mr. Garvey gets it right.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.640
Let’s see how this pattern plays out in
some other sketches.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:31.460
For example, we expect a babysitter to have
a quiet night but an unusual behavior is introduced

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:38.400
and this behavior is emotionally heightened.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.920
With a last minute reveal that offers a glimpse
into the comedic logic.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.960
In another sketch, we start with the fairly
mundane setting until we meet our unusual

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.920
companions who are intent on living their
vigilante air marshall dreams.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:22.750
The stakes are then raised well above cruising
altitude and rather than fizzling out by running

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:27.440
the pattern into the ground, we get a surprise
at the end that re-frames what we’ve just seen.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:34.280
Of course we know that sketches that don’t
follow this pattern can also be hilarious.

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:41.360
Who can deny the pure joy of Will Ferrell’s
gyrating midriff or the wondrous testicular

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:46.440
double entendre of Alec Baldwin’s baked
goods.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.420
And hell, even Key &amp; Peele occasionally bask
in the purely absurd.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.680
But comedic structure is only half the story.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:01.210
While their sketches are funny, it’s another
element of their work that ensures that they

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:06.990
are also smart: Key &amp; Peele’s commitment
to finding fundamental human truth in comedy.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:09.640
This philosophy is not an invention of Key
&amp; Peele.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:14.720
However, they both cut their comedic chops
in Chicago, a city whose comedy scene is permeated

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.790
by this guy Del Close.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:20.330
And while you might not have heard of him,
he’s responsible for training a few pretty

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:21.720
decent comedians.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:26.160
And along the way he helped write a book called
Truth in Comedy with Charna Halpern that’s

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.560
become a bible for comedians around the world.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.830
In it they argue that the best comedy comes
from mining the depths of human existence,

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:38.540
and that this creates the grounds for comedy
that is both funny AND smart- an idea we see

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:40.199
everywhere in Key &amp; Peele.

00:05:40.199 --> 00:05:44.720
Oh, and Del Close once claimed that a coven
of witches cured him of his cocaine addiction.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.540
This has nothing to do with comedy, but I think it's worth
noting.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:51.260
This commitment to truth isn't exclusive to Chicago comedians.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:56.620
As this emotionally honest style has dominated
recently, from the diner, to the breakroom,

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:57.630
to streets of Italy.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:01.920
Now does this commitment to truth mean that
good comedy always has to reflect real and

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.400
truthful situations?

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:04.100
Hell no!

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:08.560
But while a post-apocalyptic alien invasion
might not reflect the reality of the world

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:13.470
we live in [sorry for anyone currently on
acid] the core comedic idea is still grounded

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:18.000
in something fundamentally true- that Key
and Peele’s characters can instantly know

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:22.260
who is an alien by their reactions to blackness.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:32.640
The friendlier the white characters are, the
more certain they are of their alien nature.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.630
Let’s return to Mr. Garvey.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:40.800
What’s the truth that makes this sketch
so funny?

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:45.240
For a start, this sketch reverses the trope
of a well-intentioned Teach for America types

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.759
out to save a bunch of inner city kids.

00:06:47.759 --> 00:06:52.090
This role reversal produces both the immediate
comedy of Mr. Garvey’s mispronunciations,

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:56.729
and the socially conscious fact that this
is something that happens to minority students

00:06:56.729 --> 00:07:04.080
all the time, except in that case, it’s
more uncomfortable than it is funny.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.389
While Mr. Garvey’s reaction to names like
Denise and Aaron are hilarious, Key &amp; Peele

00:07:08.389 --> 00:07:13.100
are forcing us to ask a more complicated question,
what if black teachers were as culturally

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.730
incompetent as their white counterparts?

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.990
And thus we have a double pronged comedic
attack.

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:21.790
Come for the laughter, and stay for the socially
conscious criticism.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:26.639
The benefit of this mixture of comedic structure
and finding truth is that hilarious sketches

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.320
can be found in any area of life:
race, religion, the dark side of professional sports

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.080
you name it.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.380
And they often do so by relating to personal
experience.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:41.360
In their first sketch, titled "I Said Bitch"
there is a focus on the tensions found in

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.500
black masculinity between being a strong alpha
male, and a committed partner.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:59.340
The sketch heightens the limits that two friends
will go to keep up the facade of being a hard ass

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:00.480
getting further,

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:07.940
and further
away from their spouses in the process.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:15.130
And the interstellar lengths they'll go to maintain
this facade represent the actual effort men

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:17.870
will put in to prove they're one of the bros.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:22.000
And of course this sketch highlights the absurdity
of this whole macho performance.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:33.820
This theme is also explored in the expanded
Key &amp; Peele universe, as their first feature

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:38.529
film, Keanu, throws two normal suburban black
men into the world of organized crime and

00:08:38.529 --> 00:08:41.459
drug dealing,
where they have to impersonate two legendary

00:08:41.459 --> 00:08:42.459
street assassins.

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:47.980
At one point we see the one of the main characters
performing a stereotypical sort of black masculinity

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:55.460
and conversely, we see some of the hardened
gangsters brought to tears by the music of

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.560
George Michael.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:03.259
They use a similar strategy in the sketch
‘Office Homophobe’, where we see a tense

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:06.240
interaction between two officemates.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:19.480
Which leads to a serious accusation.

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:24.699
And while this sketch at first seems to set
up a tired trope in which the humor is derived

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.620
from a flamboyant gay stereotype,

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:35.760
this expectation
is upended when the homophobe’s boyfriend

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:36.520
shows up.

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:43.940
Which leads to a surprising realization for
Key’s character,

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:48.400
And the truth of this sketch?

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:52.760
Well, no matter your sexual preferences or
gender identity, you can still be an asshole.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:57.600
A more common source of comedic inspiration
has always been good old fashioned religion,

00:09:57.640 --> 00:10:00.600
and critiques of religion are not new.

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:18.600
But rather than following in the footsteps
of those who find comedy in the skewering

00:10:18.610 --> 00:10:22.240
religion for it’s irrational claims,
Key &amp; Peele find a much more human take on

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.720
religion, and in particular Christianity.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:27.640
The sketch opens with a group of earnest believers in prayer and

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:37.079
this group of Christians is rewarded with
a visit from the voice of God himself, However,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.440
once God responds to their prayers with some
practical next steps,

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.149
they become slightly less enthused.

00:10:49.149 --> 00:10:53.920
Eventually, they find a nice loophole to avoid
having to actually follow God’s command

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:55.240
of service.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:02.120
This sketch is so great because it offers
a funny critique of religion with some genuine

00:11:02.139 --> 00:11:03.670
theological nuance.

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:07.189
The joke isn’t on religion in general, or
believers in particular.

00:11:07.189 --> 00:11:11.720
Rather, it points out the hypocrisy of those
unwilling to take their own religious traditions

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:16.389
seriously when it comes to following through
on a not-so-fun obligation.

00:11:16.389 --> 00:11:20.149
This critique is actually one grounded in
Christianity itself, as the book of James

00:11:20.149 --> 00:11:23.559
famously says, “faith without works is dead.”

00:11:23.559 --> 00:11:26.690
Even your most devout Aunt couldn’t disagree
with this one.

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:30.930
An even more brutally honest social critique
can be seen in their sketch ‘Sound Off’,

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:32.540
which is set at basic training.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:36.730
While we assume that the drill sergeant is
going to lead the song in some absurd direction,

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:40.720
it ends up finding comedy in a place of harsh
realism.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:53.240
The sketch doesn’t only point out the disparity
in who ends up fighting America’s wars but

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:56.560
also the reality of what awaits soldiers coming
home from war.

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:09.440
Somehow they manage to make a sketch about
economic inequality and inadequate medical

00:12:09.459 --> 00:12:11.319
care in the military funny.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:15.600
While they have multiple sketches that use
the absurdity of football for comedic fuel

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:24.600
in Quarterback Concussion they use the trope
of the heroic quarterback to shine some light

00:12:24.619 --> 00:12:26.870
on the medical reality of the game.

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:33.119
After taking a big hit we see the quarterback
go from confused to quickly offering fictional

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:40.520
baked goods before being diagnosed by a teammate.

00:12:44.379 --> 00:12:51.640
But after seemingly getting it together in
a rousing chant of team spirit the quarterback

00:12:51.640 --> 00:12:58.800
notices a missing piece of his identity as
a Rhino and runs off.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:02.360
In an effective parody of the inspirational sports movie genre

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:05.720
Key &amp; Peele are showing us what it would look
like if inspirational sports movies accurately

00:13:05.739 --> 00:13:09.189
represented the horrific injuries suffered
by athletes.

00:13:09.189 --> 00:13:13.619
While that’s just a few examples, Key &amp; Peele’s
five seasons, plus Keanu, are filled with

00:13:13.619 --> 00:13:17.350
hours of insanely smart, and insanely funny,
sketches.

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:21.519
And because most of their work is grounded
in true human emotions and situations, rather

00:13:21.519 --> 00:13:24.720
than topical humor, these sketches really
hold up.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:29.600
But most importantly, Key &amp; Peele serves as
a reminder for the greatness of the TSA.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:36.360
So if you see any terries trying to get froggy,
you should always remember to

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:43.000
Thanks for watching guys! Peace!

